<Base title="About" description="Who makes Ancient Paths, why it has its name, and how its pages are written and checked.">
  <div class="wrap page">
    <h1>About Ancient Paths</h1>
    <p>
      Ancient Paths is made by a Christian who wants to understand where the faith came from, and to help others do the
      same.
    </p>

    <h2>Why the name?</h2>
    <p>
      The name comes from Jeremiah 6:16 in the New International Version, where the prophet calls the people to
      "ask for the ancient paths, ask where the good way is, and walk in it."
    </p>
    <p class="meta">
      Scripture quotation from the Holy Bible, New International Version®, NIV®, Copyright ©1973, 1978, 1984, 2011 by
      Biblica, Inc.® Used with permission. All rights reserved worldwide.
    </p>

    <h2>How the history pages are written</h2>
    <p>
      The history pages try to show the evidence fairly, whatever the reader believes. Each claim is tied to a source.
      Anything first reported by writers after the period is labelled as later tradition. Where scholars disagree, the
      page names the views and who holds them, rather than picking one. Where Christian tradition reads a passage in a
      particular way, the page says so and attributes it.
    </p>

    <h2>How pages are checked</h2>
    <p>
      Pages are drafted with the help of an AI and then checked against their sources. Each page shows whether that
      review has happened yet: a page marked "Draft" has not been fully reviewed, and its banner says what has been
      checked so far. The full list of sources and their licenses is on the
      <a href="/history/sources/">Sources and permissions</a> page.
    </p>
    <h3>The AI source check</h3>
    <p>
      Every page is being checked against its sources by Claude Opus 5.5, an AI model made by Anthropic. Each citation,
      quotation, date, name and figure is either checked against its source or rewritten or removed. A claim the sources
      do not support is cut back to what they do say, not left in with a label. The check is done one era at a time, and
      each era's banner says when its check was done. The check is under way: the Apostolic Age was checked in November
      2026, and the other eras have not yet been checked. When every page has been checked, this section will give the
      month the whole review was completed.
    </p>
    <p>
      Quotations and references to ancient writers are read against the full text of the public-domain translations
      named on each page. Statements about what modern scholars think are tied to a named book. Those books are in
      copyright, so the AI could not read them; the site's owner checks each statement against the book and records the
      page number. Until that is done, the statement is marked in the review ledger as not yet checked by a person.
    </p>
    <p>Sources the AI check could not read in full so far:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Raymond E. Brown, <i>An Introduction to the New Testament</i>; Henry Chadwick, <i>The Early Church</i>; Candida
        Moss, <i>The Myth of Persecution</i>; Robert Louis Wilken, <i>The Christians as the Romans Saw Them</i>; and the
        books by Hurtado, Bauckham, Dunn, Ehrman, and Becker and Reed named on the Apostolic Age page. All are in
        copyright and are on the owner's checklist.
      </li>
      <li>The Gallio inscription at Delphi, read only through published descriptions, not an edition.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Each check is recorded, with the passage that settled it, in the era's review ledger, for example the
      <a href="https://github.com/githubacct54385/ancient-paths/blob/main/docs/REVIEW-apostolic-age.md">Apostolic Age ledger</a>.
      No specialist has reviewed any page yet.
    </p>
    <p>
      Every review record is public. The project's <a href="https://github.com/githubacct54385/ancient-paths/tree/main/docs">docs folder</a>
      holds the review ledgers, which list each claim, how far it was checked, and what the check found. The plans for each
      era, and the process for checking sources, are there too.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you spot a mistake,
      <a href="https://github.com/githubacct54385/ancient-paths/issues/new/choose">please open an issue on GitHub</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
